Thanks for testing the actual Snap package and for fixing those issues in #971. I've now updated #970 against the latest `master`, so it includes the Snap changes from #971 while keeping this PR focused on native `power-profiles-daemon` handling.

While rechecking the combined flow, I found a few edge cases in the native PPD lifecycle and fixed them as well:
* an active PPD is still stopped if `powerprofilesctl` is unavailable or fails;
* an initially inactive PPD is no longer started when `--live` exits;
* PPD is only stopped after the live-mode confirmation, so cancelling there does not leave it stopped;
* an inactive-but-enabled PPD is still disabled during installation;
* a machine where `power-profiles-daemon.service` is not installed no longer gets a new systemd mask created for it.

I also changed the running-service checks to require an actual active state instead of treating an unknown state as active.

@lucasleocs let's please address above suggestion mention by Copilot.

Btw, I also noticed when automatically set that "Intel HWP Dynamic Boost" will be "on" while laptop is charging and while it's on battery. I think this is good, as I was proposed this if it wasn't the case (as in [config file we're suggesting it to be off](https://github.com/AdnanHodzic/auto-cpufreq#example-config-file-contents:~:text=%23%20hwp_dynamic_boost%20%3D%20false)).

But I'm looking at code, I'm missing what are the conditions "Intel HWP Dynamic Boost" will be "off" if ever while on e.g battery? As if it's supported it'll just be on "on" all the time unless in config file it was specified to have it off?
